NIST SP 800-63B says a verifier "SHALL compare the prospective secret
against a blocklist that contains known commonly used, expected, or
compromised passwords", and that is what happened here.

  https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html

Three kinds of password are refused.

  One built from something about this list -- its name, its domain, or
  the address the password belongs to.  Whoever tries to guess it will
  try those first, because they are printed in every message the list
  sends.

  One on the list of passwords this site has decided not to accept.

  One that has appeared in a published breach.  That does not mean this
  account has been broken into: it means the same string has been found
  in somebody else's stolen data, so it is in the lists attackers work
  from.

Length does not rescue a password that is on a list.  A long phrase
appearing in breach data is still guessed in one attempt.  Please pick
something that is yours.

Nothing about your password was sent anywhere.  The breach check sends
the first five characters of a hash that some half a million different
passwords share, and compares the answer here; the service is not told
which password was asked about.
